  "strategy": "1. Approach and Rationale\nThe Erd\u0151s minimum overlap problem seeks to find a function h(x) that minimizes the maximum overlap with its shifted versions. The current record of 0.38086 is close to the target of 0.38085, suggesting that further improvement will require escaping local optima. The proposed approach uses a multi-resolution strategy combined with periodic extension to break symmetry and avoid overlap at maximal shifts. The key intuition is that periodic functions with carefully chosen wavelengths can reduce overlaps by interleaving active and passive segments. This method directly addresses the bottleneck of the previous coordinate descent and SLSQP approaches: they tend to get stuck in shallow local minima.\n\n2. Mathematical Specification\n- Variables: h of length n, h[i] \u03f5 [0,1] with \u2211h[i] = n/2\n- Objective: C = max_{k} \u222bh(x)(1-h(x+k))dx (full mode 'full' correlation)\n- Constraints: 0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1, \u2211h = n/2\n- Periodic Extension: h(x+2) = h(x)\n- Initialization: Start with sinusoidal base functions at multiple phases\n- Optimization: Successive resolution refinement with gradient-based augmentations\n- Periodicity Parameter: p (number of periods to cover [0,2])\n- Wavelength Tuner: s-controls spacing between active segments\n\n3. Implementation Procedure\na. Initial Setup\n- If 'initial_h_values' available, start from there with n=600 samples\n- Else generate random start with uniform distribution and project feasibility\n- Parameters: p=2, s=0.4, n_iterations=300\n\nb. Phase 1: Augmented Gradient Descent\n- Compute gradient \u2207C via finite differences\n- Update rule: h = h + \u03c9\u2207C - \u03b1h + \u03b2(i-(i-1))\n- Periodic projection: ensure h(x+2) = h(x)\n- Integral restoration when deviation detected\n- Top-level optimization: maximize period density while reducing peak overlap\n\nc. Phase 2: Multi-resolution Refinement\n- Coarse-to-fine resolution: start with n=50, final n=600\n- At each resolution: replicate periodic pattern to satisfy integral constraint\n- Wavelength adaptation: if overlap fraction exceeds 0.4, increase s by 10%\n- Progressive clipping: ensure values stay [0,1] without destroying integral\n\nd. Phase 3:-periodic Symmetry Breaking\n- For each candidate: generate 5 rotated versions (shift of \u03c0/4, \u03c0)\n- Break symmetry by ensuring no periodic translation improvement\n- Select candidate with absolute minimum C\n\ne. Candidate Validation\n- Clip values to [0,1]\n- Project to satisfy \u2211h = n/2\n- Compute full correlation to find C\n- Confirm convergence by residual check ||\u0394h|| < tol\n\n4. Search Choices and Variations\na. Default Configuration\n- Resolution sweep: n=100 \u2192 n=200 \u2192 ... until n=600\n- Initial period density: 4-periods per [0,2]\n- Example parameters: p \u2208 [4,8], s \u2208 [0.35,0.45]\n\nb. Adaptive Variation\n- When peak overlap > 0.4: increase s by 5% (no. of cycles)\n- When C improvement stagnates > 100 iterations: increase top_k by 10%\n\nc. Periodicity Tamer\n- If periodicity not helpful (C scales linearly): switch to random harmonic perturbation\n\n5. Compute Budget and Robustness\n- Phase 1: Dominant cost is finite-difference Hessian evaluation (n\u00b2)\n- Phase 2: Ores compute time \u2192 n_points \u2264 500 before refinement\n- Deadline monitoring: stop phase 3 if >20% budget spent\n\n6. Validation and Return Contract\n- Feasibility check: confirm 0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1, \u2211h = n/2\n- Objective check: compute correlation in integer arithmetic\n- L1 residual < 1e-5 confirms convergence\n- Return (h_values, c5_bound, n_points)",
